WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.440
Two plots hold the same four species.

2
00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:09.460
Each plot has 100 individuals.

3
00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:13.140
In plot A, every species has 25.

4
00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:18.880
In plot B, the counts are 40, 40, 10 and 10.

5
00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:21.280
Is one plot more diverse?

6
00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:24.700
The answer depends on what you decide to count.

7
00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:27.380
Start with richness.

8
00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:30.620
Richness is the number of species present.

9
00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:34.060
Both plots have four, so richness is tied.

10
00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:37.160
But richness ignores abundance.

11
00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:42.460
It cannot see that plot B has two common species and two rare ones.

12
00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:45.760
To see that, use relative abundance.

13
00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:50.380
That is the share of all individuals that belong to each species.

14
00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:54.200
In plot A, every share is 0.25.

15
00:00:55.333 --> 00:00:57.513
Now try Simpson concentration.

16
00:00:58.293 --> 00:01:02.873
Pull two individuals at random, putting the first back before the second.

17
00:01:03.833 --> 00:01:08.833
Simpson concentration, called lambda, is the chance that both belong to the same

18
00:01:08.833 --> 00:01:12.733
species. In plot A, lambda is 0.25.

19
00:01:13.393 --> 00:01:15.413
In plot B, it is 0.34.

20
00:01:16.733 --> 00:01:19.113
Lambda runs the opposite way from the other measures.

21
00:01:19.113 --> 00:01:21.773
It rises as one species dominates.

22
00:01:22.683 --> 00:01:26.263
Nats and probabilities are hard to compare.

23
00:01:26.703 --> 00:01:30.483
So convert each measure to an effective number of species.

24
00:01:31.123 --> 00:01:35.763
That is the number of equally common species that would give the same value.

25
00:01:36.683 --> 00:01:41.243
For plot A, the answer is 4.00 by every measure.

26
00:01:42.143 --> 00:01:46.383
For plot B, Shannon entropy gives 3.30 species.

27
00:01:47.283 --> 00:01:49.743
Inverse Simpson gives 2.94.

28
00:01:50.633 --> 00:01:55.613
Notice the pattern in plot B. Order zero gives four species.

29
00:01:56.213 --> 00:01:58.353
The next order gives 3.3.

30
00:01:58.993 --> 00:02:01.533
The one after gives 2.94.

31
00:02:02.893 --> 00:02:05.413
Higher orders give more weight to common species.

32
00:02:05.933 --> 00:02:10.233
So plot B's two rare species count for less as the order rises.

33
00:02:11.513 --> 00:02:14.253
Evenness is inverse Simpson divided by richness.

34
00:02:15.033 --> 00:02:19.573
It is one for plot A and 0.735 for plot B.

35
00:02:20.317 --> 00:02:23.617
Now swap the labels on plot B's species.

36
00:02:24.357 --> 00:02:25.757
Every number stays the same.

37
00:02:26.417 --> 00:02:29.097
No index sees which species are present.

38
00:02:29.857 --> 00:02:33.797
Two plots with identical indices can share no species at all.

39
00:02:34.637 --> 00:02:36.057
Each measure misses something.

40
00:02:36.817 --> 00:02:38.257
Richness ignores abundance.

41
00:02:39.197 --> 00:02:41.177
Higher orders discount rare species.

42
00:02:42.057 --> 00:02:45.397
So always name the measure you used, and say what it misses.
